WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.820
When you wear the rings of power, you win
or you die.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.240
Wait, that's not right.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.940
Game of Thrones and Lord of the Rings are
two different shows.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.630
Still, they hold a surprising connection.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.789
While the entire genre of fantasy fiction
owes a huge debt to J.R.R.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:20.550
Tolkien, the creation of Amazon's first foray
into Middle-earth, "The Lord of the Rings:

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:25.160
The Rings of Power," might be a by-product
of the success of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.250
HBO's adaptation of George R.R.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.849
Martin's "A Song of Ice and Fire" book series
was the ultimate fantasy saga of the 2010s,

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:37.110
inspiring networks and streaming services
to acquire the rights to popular genre IPs.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.800
Without "Game of Thrones," shows such as "The
Witcher" and "The Wheel of Time" might not exist.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.030
Of course, comparisons between the aforementioned
franchises were always inevitable considering

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.460
that Amazon's "The Rings of Power" debuted
shortly after the premiere of "House of the

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.800
Dragon" on HBO.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.640
Even Martin knows that viewers will pit the
shows against each other in a battle of fantasy

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.740
warfare, but he hopes that people can enjoy
both shows on their own accord.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.740
He told the "Independent,"

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.820
"I hope both shows succeed.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.300
I'm competitive enough.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.960
I hope we succeed more.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.250
If they win six Emmys, and I hope they do,
I hope we win seven."

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.170
At the end of the day, the "Lord of the Rings"
and "Game of Thrones" franchises are unique

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.120
entities that bring their own spins to fantasy
fare.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.840
However, that doesn't mean they don't share
some common elements that enable perfectly

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.200
understandable comparisons to be made.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:32.260
Robert Aramayo is the force that binds together
"The Rings Of Power" and "Game of Thrones" franchises.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.170
The 29-year-old English actor has developed
a knack for playing popular characters on

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:42.820
fantasy series, or to be more specific, playing
the younger versions of beloved, established characters.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.060
Aramayo rose to fame by portraying a young
Ned Stark during the sixth season of "Game of Thrones."

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:54.450
He appeared in a series of flashback sequences
that shed some light on Jon Snow's family history.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.940
"No.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.340
Now it ends."

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.799
He did an admirable job of honoring the legacy
of Sean Bean, who played the House of Stark

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:03.980
leader during his middle-aged years.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:09.200
Of course, one does not simply forget that
Sean Bean connected The Lord of the Rings

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:15.450
and Game of Thrones when it premiered in 2011
– so Ned Stark is the true connector between

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.750
George RR Martin and JRR Tolkien.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:23.260
In "The Rings of Power," the Englishman plays
a younger version of Elrond, the legendary

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:28.870
half-elven character who's appeared in every
onscreen "Lord of the Rings" project to date.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.629
The character, who was brought to life by
Hugo Weaving in "Lord of the Rings" and "The

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:37.329
Hobbit," will have a key role to play in the
Amazon series, which was what attracted Aramayo

00:02:37.329 --> 00:02:39.989
to the fantasy project in the first place.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:44.859
The Elrond we meet in "The Lord of the Rings:
The Rings of Power" is slightly different

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:48.680
from the wise and storied warrior we see in
Peter Jackson's movies.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.470
Here, he's not quite the strong, stubborn,
commanding leader that he went on to become.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:57.849
For a start, he's relatively uninterested
in attending council meetings and concerning

00:02:57.849 --> 00:02:59.689
himself with political matters.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:04.279
Additionally, he's not on the best terms with
some of the elves' allies, though he is able

00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:09.219
to use his charm and cunning to get what he
wants during moments of political persuasion.

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:13.579
While speaking to "Variety," Robert Aramayo
revealed that bringing young Elrond to fruition

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:17.809
was exciting as it meant exploring uncharted
territory in some ways.

00:03:17.809 --> 00:03:22.389
Furthermore, he doesn't think that "Game of
Thrones" and "The Rings of Power" are anything

00:03:22.389 --> 00:03:25.840
alike, with the exception of being fantasy
series that he worked on.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:26.840
He said,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.809
"They're very, very different shows.

00:03:28.809 --> 00:03:33.809
My job on [Game of Thrones] and my job on
[The Rings of Power] were very different."

00:03:33.809 --> 00:03:37.709
According to the actor, "Games of Thrones"
allowed him to draw upon material that was

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:39.449
already there for inspiration.

00:03:39.449 --> 00:03:44.340
However, since the First and Second Ages of
Middle-earth are open to interpretation in

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:50.254
many ways, "The Rings of Power" allowed him
to essentially build a character from scratch.

